---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Art Style - How Pixel Art Makes It Scary';
const description =
  'Perfect Garbage\'s official art devlog on Grave Seasons: the horror mood board, why pixel art limits help the dread, and Elliott\'s warning that the world changes.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Art Style', url: '/art-style/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.blumhouseMedia, SOURCES.steam];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.monsterAttack.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Art - official devlog</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      The Art of Grave Seasons
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Perfect Garbage published a devlog called "The Art of Grave Seasons" in which four members of
      the art team explain how they make isometric pixel art unsettling. It contains one line that is
      arguably a gameplay tip in disguise. Everything on this page comes from that devlog and the
      official media gallery.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.monsterAttack.src} alt={ASSETS.monsterAttack.alt} width={ASSETS.monsterAttack.width} height={ASSETS.monsterAttack.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.monsterAttack.credit}</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>Who makes the art</h2>
      <p>
        The devlog names four members of the art team directly:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mima</strong> - Lead Pixel Artist</li>
        <li><strong>Elliott</strong> - Pixel Art Direction</li>
        <li><strong>Liz</strong> - Animation Lead</li>
        <li><strong>Suvi</strong> - Art and Audio Direction</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Suvi's dual art-and-audio role is worth noting, because it lines up with the
        <a href="/soundtrack/">soundtrack</a>, where Suvi Savikko is credited on the music side too.
        The same person is shaping how Ashenridge looks and how it sounds.
      </p>

      <h2>The horror mood board was crowdsourced from the team</h2>
      <p>
        This is the most concrete production detail in the post. To find the right kind of dread, the
        team was asked to submit <strong>the most upsetting moments they had ever experienced in
        horror media</strong> - films, games, anything that genuinely stuck with them. Suvi sorted
        through the whole pile, and the result was what the studio calls "a disturbingly lovely mood
        board."
      </p>
      <p>
        So the horror in Grave Seasons is not sourced from a single reference work. It is assembled
        from whatever individually frightened the people making it, which is a reasonable explanation
        for why the tone is described as eerie rather than as an homage to any one thing.
      </p>

      <h2>Why cozy first, horror second</h2>
      <p>
        Perfect Garbage describe themselves as "huge fans of farming sims," and the pitch was to take
        the genre's traditional brightness - "sunshine, smiles, and cozy cottages" - and layer
        something darker underneath, "something that makes you second guess what may be hiding beneath
        the surface."
      </p>
      <p>
        Mima puts the contrast plainly: <em>"You wouldn't expect something eerie happening in a nice
        little village with cute round chickens, you know?"</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the design thesis in one sentence. The cuteness is not decoration applied on top of a
        horror game; it is the mechanism that makes the horror land.
      </p>

      <h2>Pixel art as a horror advantage, not a compromise</h2>
      <p>
        The team frames the medium's limits as the point. Mima again: <em>"One of my favorite things
        about working in pixel art is the limitation of the medium. Fitting all of the concepts of
        creepiness in just a tiny space is very fascinating."</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        The reasoning the devlog gives is that small canvases <strong>invite your imagination to fill
        the gaps</strong>. The example they use: catching a glimpse of a shadowy figure and being
        unable to tell what it is - a ghost, a curtain, or the light playing tricks. They are
        deliberately building that uncertainty into the art so your own mind supplies the fear.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-fog">
        Worth being precise: this is the team describing intent. Whether a given shadow in the
        finished game is a real threat or set dressing is exactly what they are declining to tell you,
        and we are not going to pretend to know either.
      </p>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img src={ASSETS.hari.src} alt={ASSETS.hari.alt} width={ASSETS.hari.width} height={ASSETS.hari.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.hari.credit}</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The line that reads like a gameplay hint</h2>
      <p>
        Elliott, on pixel art direction, says this:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          "You should certainly be keeping an eye on the world and remembering what it looked like
          yesterday, because it's not always going to stay the same."
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Read that as an art-direction comment and it is a note about environmental storytelling. Read
        it as advice and it is the closest thing to an official investigation tip that has been
        published: <strong>the environment changes between days, and noticing the change is on you</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        It fits what is already confirmed elsewhere. Steam states a murder happens each month across a
        one-year run, and the <a href="/day-and-night/">day and night cycle</a> devlog confirms there is
        no forced bedtime, so you can be out in the world at hours when it looks different. A world
        that visibly changes day to day is the kind of system that rewards paying attention rather than
        following a checklist.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-fog">
        What Elliott does <em>not</em> say: what changes, how often, whether the changes are clues,
        or whether they are tied to the killer. Any guide claiming to list "environmental clues to
        watch" before launch is inventing them.
      </p>

      <h2>Ashenridge is described as watching you</h2>
      <p>
        The devlog closes on the village itself. Ashenridge is called "our little seaside village at
        the heart of it all," and the team say it is "more than just a setting to us - it's alive,
        breathing, and quietly watching."
      </p>
      <p>
        The seaside detail is consistent with the <a href="/map-of-ashenridge/">map of Ashenridge</a>,
        which covers the coast alongside the farm, the forest, and the town's two districts.
      </p>

      <h2>What this changes about how you play</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Treat the art as information. The team's stated goal is that ambiguity in a small sprite does
          the scaring, so a shape you cannot identify is a deliberate design choice.
        </li>
        <li>
          Compare days. Elliott's line is the only official encouragement to track how the world looked
          previously, and it came from the art team rather than a systems post.
        </li>
        <li>
          Expect the cozy surface to stay cozy. The stated approach is contrast, not a mid-game tonal
          switch - the chickens keep being cute while something is wrong underneath.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Confirmed vs not confirmed</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Confirmed:</strong> the four named art-team members and their roles; the team-sourced
        horror mood board and Suvi's role compiling it; the studio's stated intent to use pixel art
        ambiguity for horror; Elliott's statement that the world does not always stay the same;
        Ashenridge as a seaside village described as alive and watching.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Not confirmed:</strong> what specifically changes in the world or how often; whether
        those changes are investigative clues; any link between visual changes and the killer; the
        full size of the art team beyond the four people named.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the systems these visuals sit on, see the <a href="/day-and-night/">day and night cycle</a>
        and <a href="/character-customization/">character customization</a>, which covers the sprite and
        animation workload behind every wearable. For spoiler-safe preparation, see our
        <a href="/tips/">tips page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
